     "level": "I",
     "q": "DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP: what's the difference?",
     "a": "`DELETE` removes chosen rows, is logged row by row, can be rolled back and fires triggers. `TRUNCATE` removes all rows at once, is fast and minimally logged, and often can't be rolled back (it's DDL in many databases). `DROP` removes the whole table: structure, data, indexes and permissions.",
     "e": "SQLite has no TRUNCATE; `DELETE FROM t` without WHERE is optimised to do the same.",
     "code": "-- PostgreSQL / MySQL / Snowflake\nDELETE FROM staging_orders WHERE loaded_at < '2024-01-01';\nTRUNCATE TABLE staging_orders;\nDROP TABLE staging_orders;",
